Subject: [PATCH] sycl: port CUDA's model-specific fusions and topk-moe expert groups

Local port of the rest of the CUDA graph fusions that apply to models an Arc card runs:

- SCALE -> TANH -> SCALE (logit softcap) and RELU -> SQR (squared ReLU) as single elementwise kernels.
- MUL -> SIN -> SQR -> MUL -> ADD, the snake activation of audio decoders, x [T, C] with per-channel
  a and 1/b (CUDA's snake fusion; F32, F16 and BF16 activations).
- SCALE -> SIGMOID -> SCALE feeding DSV4_HC_POST's post weights (qwen4exp's hc_combine): the hc_post
  kernel computes gate_out * sigmoid(gate_in * post) itself.
- SSM_SCAN + the CPY that scatters its state snapshots into the cache: the mamba-2 group scan writes
  the final state and the rollback snapshots straight into the cache view and skips its snapshot tail
  (CUDA's ggml_cuda_try_ssm_scan_cache_fusion; one sequence, d_state 128 / 256 as the SYCL kernel).
- topk-moe: sqrt(softplus) gating (DeepSeek-V4) and DeepSeek-V3's expert-group masking (keep the
  n_group_used groups with the best sum of their top two experts), up to WARP_SIZE groups, ported from
  CUDA's topk_moe_cuda and its group pattern matcher. The masking is its own kernel instantiation, so
  routers without groups keep their registers. At 256 / 512 experts it spilled 1.4-9.1 KB per thread at
  128 registers on acm-g10; a router runs one sub-group per token and has no occupancy to lose, so there
  it takes the 256-register file on Intel GPUs with XMX (no spill at 256 experts, 1.0-1.2 KB at 512).

Not ported: CUDA's shared-expert fusion, which only fires when the shared expert has the routed
experts' type and shape (Qwen2-MoE and DeepSeek-V2-Lite size it differently, and a quant mix can give
it another type) and saves one launch, and the opt-in multi-stream Q/K/V concurrency (GGML_CUDA_GRAPH_OPT).

